    "question": "In triangle $ABC$, $\\angle A = 40^\\circ$, $\\angle B = (2x)^\\circ$ and $\\angle C = (x + 20)^\\circ$.\nFind the measure of the angle that is opposite the longest side.",
    "level": "ordinary",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Find $x$",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "The angles in a triangle add up to $180^\\circ$."
        },
        {
         "tex": "40 + 2x + (x + 20) &= 180",
         "sym": "Eq(40 + 2*x + (x + 20), 180)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "3x + 60 &= 180",
         "sym": "Eq(3*x + 60, 180)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= 40",
         "sym": "Eq(x, 40)"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Find the angles",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "\\angle B &= 2(40) = 80^\\circ",
         "sym": "2*40",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\angle C &= 40 + 20 = 60^\\circ",
         "sym": "40 + 20",
         "new": true
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Use Theorem 7",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "80 &> 60 > 40",
         "sym": "And(80 > 60, 60 > 40)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "The greatest side is opposite the greatest angle, so the angle opposite the longest side is $\\angle B$."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "80^\\circ"
    },
    "finalSym": "80",
    "answerSym": "2*(180 - 40 - 20)/3"
   }
  ]
